get_timeline_transcript Answers every word HEARD on the timeline (or inside the nest set_scope has open), in timeline order, nested clips included: words is [{text, start, end, clip, track, media, engine, filler, speaker?, editable, why?, id, cut_start, cut_end}], times in TIMELINE ticks (600000 per second), ordered by start, then end, then the front track first. A clip that is muted, soloed out or at volume 0 is not heard and gives no words. track is the timeline track the word plays on (a nested word's is its nest clip's). editable is false, with why, for a word inside a nest, under a transition or on a locked track. id {nest_path, media, source, k} names the word across edits: the nest clips it plays through, its media, its start in the media's SOURCE ticks as the recogniser put it and k, counting earlier words with the same three (0 unless one piece of the source plays twice). An engine apple word's start is where its sound begins, read from the media's voice and waveform caches; until the voice cache exists it is the recogniser's, often early, and notes says so. cut_start and cut_end are the range the Transcript tab cuts that word by when it alone is deleted, snapped to frames: from the word's onset (where its sound starts, read from the voice cache) to the next word's onset, at most 1 s past the word's end, widened over any other track's word it would cut into. To cut a run of words, call ripple_delete_ranges once with one range from its first word's cut_start to its last word's cut_end. pauses is [{start, end, cut_start, cut_end, clip, track, media, follows, precedes}]: each silence over 1 s between two words of one clip, read from the voice cache, start and end where it runs, follows and precedes the ids of the words before and after it, one row per pause whose word before is in words; cut_start and cut_end are what the Transcript tab cuts when that pause is deleted (0.2 s kept at each end, snapped to frames): shorten one with ripple_delete_ranges [[cut_star